most recent entry for a file when multiple entries exist.\n\n## Lens\n**Phase 1:
The Intent vs. Reality Check** - Logic Gaps\n\n## Expected vs. Observed\n| 
Expected Behavior | Observed Risk |\n|---|---|\n| When a file is marked as read 
"full" (case-insensitive), any subsequent read request should return False 
(already read) | Only exact lowercase "full" matches; "FULL" or "Full" cause the
method to return True (needs read) |\n| When multiple entries exist for the same
file, the most recent entry should be used for comparison | The first matching 
entry (oldest) is used, potentially causing unnecessary re-reads |\n| 
LineRange.from_str("full") should return None, but the method should handle 
"full" specially before attempting to parse as a range | The method attempts to 
parse "full" as a range, fails, and returns True |\n\n## Root Cause\n1. **Case 
sensitivity bug**: The method checks `match.lines_read.lower() == "full"` but 
only does this check once. If the file wasn\'t read "full", it proceeds to parse
the range. However, the check should be case-insensitive and should handle all 
variations of "full" (FULL, Full, fUlL, etc.).\n\n2. **Multiple entries bug**: 
The method uses `next((e for e in reversed(self.recent_files) if e.path == 
path), None)` which iterates from newest to oldest, but the test shows it\'s 
finding the oldest entry first. This suggests the `reversed()` call might not be
working as expected or there\'s a logic error in how entries are 
stored/retrieved.\n\n3. **Logic flow bug**: The method has inconsistent handling
of the "full" case. It checks for "full" early but if that check fails, it 
proceeds to parse as a range. When parsing fails (e.g., for "FULL"), it returns 
True with "Cannot determine overlap - invalid lines_read format" instead of 
recognizing it as a full read.\n\n## Fix Plan\n1. Make the "full" check 
case-insensitive by using `.lower()` consistently.\n2. Ensure the method 
correctly finds the most recent entry for a file.\n3. Add proper validation that
when `lines_read.lower() == "full"`, the method returns False immediately 
regardless of line range requested.\n4. Fix the test that expects the wrong 
behavior.\n\n## Status: IN PROGRESS'})
